Bug report CD-1510: "I have exactly 1000 EXP and my profile still says Level 4. The level table says Level 5 starts at 1000."

// levels.ts
const LEVEL_STARTS = [0, 100, 300, 600, 1000]; // Level 1..5

export function levelFromExp(exp: number): number {
  let level = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i < LEVEL_STARTS.length; i++) {
    if (exp > LEVEL_STARTS[i]) level = i + 1;
  }
  return level;
}

The only test of the profile page is an end-to-end test that signs up a new learner (0 EXP) and checks that the page shows "Level 1".

Statement: "A unit test that calls levelFromExp(1000) and expects 5 would have caught this bug, without a database, a server or a browser."

Bug report CD-1527: "After double-clicking Enroll, I see the course twice in My Courses." Production database:

SELECT user_id, course_id, count(*) FROM enrollments GROUP BY 1, 2 HAVING count(*) > 1;
 user_id   | course_id  | count
 u_88213   | sql-basics |     2

The service:

export async function enrollLearner(repo: EnrollmentRepo, userId: string, courseId: string) {
  if (await repo.exists(userId, courseId)) return;   // SELECT 1 FROM enrollments WHERE ...
  await repo.insert(userId, courseId);              // INSERT INTO enrollments ...
}

The enrollments table has no unique constraint on (user_id, course_id). The existing test uses an in-memory fake:

it("does not enroll twice", async () => {
  const repo = new InMemoryEnrollmentRepo();
  await enrollLearner(repo, "u1", "sql-basics");
  await enrollLearner(repo, "u1", "sql-basics");
  expect(await repo.count("u1", "sql-basics")).toBe(1);
}); // passes

Statement: "Another unit test with the in-memory fake repository, sending the two enroll calls at the same time with Promise.all, is the cheapest test that reliably catches this bug."
